theory RHTNCeremony
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN presence ceremony -- symbolic analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHAT THIS FILE IS
  The review plan's Stage 1.1, highest-value target: "An adversary not
  physically present cannot produce a [presence] record a third party
  accepts."  A presence record (design Section 7-8, wire Section 4.5) is the
  signed artifact of a face-to-face ceremony: two participants and a set of
  witnesses co-sign that a meeting happened.  The property proved here: a
  record a third party accepts implies the two named participants were
  CO-PRESENT -- an adversary who was not in the room cannot manufacture one,
  short of stealing a participant's key.

  THE CENTRAL MODELLING DECISION: co-presence as an AXIOM.
  Physical co-location is not a protocol mechanism -- it is the real-world
  fact the whole design meters trust against (design Section 1.2.1).  A
  symbolic prover has no notion of physical space, so we introduce
  co-presence as a restricted resource:
    * a `Meet` rule produces a CoPresent fact for a set of parties, and a
      restriction lets it fire only for genuinely distinct parties;
    * the ceremony's binding signatures can only be produced by a party
      that holds a CoPresent token for that ceremony.
  The theorem is then HONEST about its dependency: it proves that the
  protocol admits a record ONLY when the co-presence axiom was invoked (or a
  key was stolen).  It does not prove co-presence is unforgeable in the
  physical world -- nothing symbolic could.  This is exactly the plan's
  instruction: "Model the ceremony channel as one the adversary can only
  use when a co-location fact holds ... the target property becomes
  provable relative to that axiom, which is honest."

  Reading conventions (rule shape, sorts, trace, temporal logic) are in
  attach.spthy; new idioms are explained where they first appear.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: signing

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IDENTITIES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Participants and witnesses are ordinary nodes with long-term signing keys.
  A single Register rule serves all roles -- role is a matter of what a node
  DOES in a ceremony, not a separate kind of identity (design Section 3).
*/

rule Register:
    [ Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ Reg($A) ]->
    [ !Ltk($A, ~sk), !Pk($A, pk(~sk)), Out(pk(~sk)) ]

rule Compromise:
    [ !Ltk($A, ~sk) ]
  --[ Compromised($A) ]->
    [ Out(~sk) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE CO-PRESENCE AXIOM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A ceremony is identified by a fresh ceremony token ~cid (design Section
  7.4.1's pre-commitment plays this role: a unique per-ceremony value).
  The Meet rule mints CoPresent facts for the two participants and one
  witness, all sharing the same ~cid, and stamps a MeetHappened action
  recording exactly WHO was physically together.

  A CoPresent fact is LINEAR and each is CONSUMED by the party using it to
  sign, so a single meeting authorises a single ceremony's signatures --
  the adversary cannot recycle one co-presence token across many forged
  records.

  The restriction DistinctParties forbids the degenerate "meeting" of a
  party with itself, mirroring the design's rule that participants and
  witnesses are distinct roles (wire Section 3.2: a participant is not its
  own witness; the two participants are distinct).
*/

rule Meet:
    [ Fr(~cid) ]                       // a fresh ceremony identifier
  --[ MeetHappened($P1, $P2, $W, ~cid) // trace: who was physically present
    , DistinctParties($P1, $P2, $W) ]->
    [ CoPresent($P1, ~cid)             // each attendee gets a co-presence token
    , CoPresent($P2, ~cid)
    , CoPresent($W, ~cid)
    , !Ceremony($P1, $P2, $W, ~cid) ]  // persistent record of the roster

restriction DistinctParties:
  "All a b w #i. DistinctParties(a, b, w) @ #i
     ==> not(a = b) & not(a = w) & not(b = w)"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE CEREMONY SIGNATURES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A presence record binds: the two participants and the witness each sign
  the ceremony (design Section 8.1, wire Section 4.5 -- the envelope carries
  participant and witness signatures over the record body).  We model the
  record body as <'presence', P1, P2, W, cid>: the roster plus the ceremony
  id.  Each signer's rule CONSUMES its CoPresent token for that cid, so a
  signature can only be produced by a party that was actually present (or,
  via Compromise, by a thief holding that party's key -- the modelled
  exception).

  Each signer emits its signature to the network; the verifier (below)
  collects all three and checks them.
*/

// A participant signs the ceremony it attended.
rule Participant_Sign:
    [ !Ltk($P, ~sk)
    , CoPresent($P, cid)               // consumed: this party was present
    , !Ceremony($P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]   // the roster this cid belongs to
  --[ ParticipantSigned($P, $P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]->
    [ Out(sign(<'presence', $P1, $P2, $W, cid>, ~sk)) ]

// A witness signs the ceremony it observed.
rule Witness_Sign:
    [ !Ltk($W, ~sk)
    , CoPresent($W, cid)               // consumed: the witness was present
    , !Ceremony($P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]
  --[ WitnessSigned($W, $P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]->
    [ Out(sign(<'presence', $P1, $P2, $W, cid>, ~sk)) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ACCEPTING A RECORD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A third party (a later evaluator) accepts a presence record for roster
  <P1,P2,W,cid> when it has all three binding signatures, each verifying
  against the named identity's public key.  This is wire Section 4.5's
  structural check reduced to its authenticating core: the record is only
  as good as the signatures over it, and every one must verify.
*/

rule Accept_Record:
    [ !Pk($P1, pk1), !Pk($P2, pk2), !Pk($W, pkw)
    , In(sig1), In(sig2), In(sigw) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig1, <'presence', $P1, $P2, $W, cid>, pk1), true)
    , Eq(verify(sig2, <'presence', $P1, $P2, $W, cid>, pk2), true)
    , Eq(verify(sigw, <'presence', $P1, $P2, $W, cid>, pkw), true)
    , RecordAccepted($P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]->
    [ ]

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// SANITY: an honest ceremony produces an acceptable record.  Without this
// the security lemma could be vacuously true.  (This is the executability
// check the plan-adjacent notes flag as the first thing to write.)
lemma ceremony_is_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 W cid #i. RecordAccepted(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #i"

// THE MAIN PROPERTY (Stage 1.1).  Every accepted record for roster
// <P1,P2,W,cid> implies a real meeting of exactly those parties under that
// ceremony id -- UNLESS one of the named parties' keys was compromised.
// Because each binding signature consumes a CoPresent token, and CoPresent
// tokens come only from Meet (which stamps MeetHappened for the same
// roster and cid), a record cannot exist without the meeting having
// happened.  The compromise carve-out is the design's own residual: a
// thief with a participant's key can sign as them (design Section 18.3),
// which is theft, not remote forgery.
//
// This is the plan's target verbatim: "An adversary not physically present
// cannot produce a record a third party accepts" -- relative to the
// co-presence axiom, and modulo key theft, both stated.
lemma presence_requires_copresence:
  "All P1 P2 W cid #i.
     RecordAccepted(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex #m. MeetHappened(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #m & #m < #i)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(P1) @ #k)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(P2) @ #k)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(W) @ #k) )"

// STRONGER: no record without co-presence, isolating the no-theft case.
// With NO key compromise anywhere, an accepted record requires the
// meeting.  This is the pure form of the physical-presence guarantee: an
// uncompromised set of identities cannot have a record fabricated about
// them by any network adversary.
lemma no_remote_forgery:
  "All P1 P2 W cid #i.
     ( RecordAccepted(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #i
     & not(Ex A #k. Compromised(A) @ #k) )
     ==> (Ex #m. MeetHappened(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #m & #m < #i)"

// FRESHNESS / NO CEREMONY-ID REUSE: each accepted record's ceremony id
// traces to a distinct Meet.  Two different accepted rosters cannot share
// one meeting -- the CoPresent tokens for a cid bind to one roster only.
// (This guards against a transplant where one meeting's signatures are
// re-used to vouch for a different pairing.)
lemma copresence_binds_one_roster:
  "All P1 P2 W cid Q1 Q2 X #i #j.
     ( RecordAccepted(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #i
     & RecordAccepted(Q1, Q2, X, cid) @ #j
     & not(Ex A #k. Compromised(A) @ #k) )
     ==> ( P1 = Q1 & P2 = Q2 & W = X )"

end
